@chapter Package fsimp

@node Introduction to package fsimp, Functions and Variables for fsimp, Top, Top
@section Introduction to package fsimp

The @code{fsimp} package provides a 'fullsimp' simplification function
for expressions.  This code was adapted from David Scherfgen's
dssimp.mac code posted on the Maxima discussion list.

This code implements a simplification routine for Maxima CAS
expressions using the core simplification routines for expressions
invovling trigonometric, logarithmic, exponential, polynomial and
other functions.

To install the package with mxpm, run @code{mxpm install --path} on a
copy of the repository. Without mxpm, copy the repository into a
directory under @code{~/.maxima}, which Maxima 5.47 and later search,
or add its directory to @code{file_search_maxima} and
@code{file_search_lisp}, or load it by its full path. Then

@code{load("fullsimp");}

loads the package. @code{load("fsimp")} also works. To simplify an
expression @code{expr} call

@code{fullsimp(expr);}

The functions, variables and rules the package defines all start with
@code{fs}, apart from @code{fullsimp} itself, so loading it leaves the
names a user works with alone. An input with @code{gamma} or a factorial
loads Maxima's @code{gamma_simp} share package, which defines functions
of its own in the session; excluding its routines, as in
@code{fullsimp(e, gamma_simp, factorial_simp)}, keeps it from loading.

Loading the package also undoes one change that Maxima itself makes.
Maxima's @code{trigsimp}, which @code{fullsimp} uses, declares @code{a} as
a pattern variable with @code{matchdeclare} when it first loads, so that
@code{a} matches any expression in rules the user later defines with
@code{defrule}, @code{tellsimp} or @code{let}. Loading @code{fsimp.mac}
loads @code{trigsimp} at once and removes that declaration, unless
@code{a} already had one. The rules of @code{trigsimp} are compiled by
then, so @code{trigsimp} works as before. A user who wants @code{a} as a
pattern variable can declare it again with @code{matchdeclare}.

The algorithm begins by searching through an expression for e.g. trig,
log, exp, etc. and choosing a set of core simplification
routines. Then it runs a poor-man's kind of simulated annealing
routine. It's poor-man's because it's not really simulated annealing,
but while it cycles through various core simplification routines
(several times) it allows the intermediate expression to become
slightly larger if it ultimately becomes smaller. The output is always
the smallest size expression, where the size is computed by the lisp
@code{conssize} function plus the string length of the expression, with
each variable, and each name of a function Maxima has no simplifier for
(such as an undefined @code{f}), counted as one character. Renaming
variables or such functions therefore leaves the size of every
expression unchanged.

By default the search keeps a small pool of candidates instead of a
single one (a beam search). The input expression always stays in the
pool, so every routine is applied to the original as well as to the
intermediate results, next to the cheapest candidates found so far and
one candidate that is allowed to be larger. Each round applies every
routine to every new member of the pool, and the search stops when a
round adds nothing new. The result is never larger than what any single
routine produces from the input. The original single-candidate sweep is
still available. See @code{fs_search}, @code{fs_beam_width} and
@code{fs_max_rounds}.

When the expression is a sum or product of parts that share no
variables, such as a polynomial in @code{a} and @code{b} plus a
trigonometric expression in @code{x}, each part is simplified on its
own, so a routine suited to one part never runs on the others. See
@code{fs_split}.

The smallest form is not always the one wanted. The option
@code{fs_prefer} lists form preferences, such as a denominator without
radicals or a factored polynomial, that count for more than size. See
@code{fs_prefer} and @code{fs_rank}.

Some core routines, such as @code{radcan}, @code{rootscontract} and
@code{logcontract}, rewrite expressions in ways that are only valid for
part of the complex plane. For example @code{radcan} turns
@code{sqrt(x^2+2*x+1)} into @code{x+1}, which is wrong for @code{x < -1}.
So @code{fullsimp} checks every candidate against the input expression
numerically, at a fixed set of test points, and discards any candidate
that disagrees at a point. The test points cover positive and negative
reals and all four complex quadrants, and they respect the
@code{assume} database and @code{declare}d features: after
@code{assume(x > -1)}, @code{fullsimp(sqrt(x^2+2*x+1))} returns
@code{x+1}. See @code{fs_check}.

The rtest files @code{rtest_fsimp1.mac} to @code{rtest_fsimp3.mac},
@code{rtest_fsimp_check.mac}, @code{rtest_fsimp_beam.mac},
@code{rtest_fsimp_split.mac} and @code{rtest_fsimp_prefer.mac} test the
package. @code{tests/run_rtests.sh}
runs each file in its own Maxima process and reports pass counts and
timings. @code{tests/fsimp_audit_rtests.mac} checks that every expected result
in the rtest files equals its input numerically, independently of
@code{fs_check}. @code{tests/fsimp_compare_single.mac} checks that
@code{fullsimp} is never costlier than any single routine applied to the
input, and @code{tests/fsimp_check_collisions.mac} that binding a variable
outside @code{fullsimp} never changes its result, with and without form
preferences.

E. Majzoub.

@node Functions and Variables for fsimp, , Introduction to package fsimp, Top
@section Functions and Variables for fsimp

@defvr {Variable} fsdebug
Setting the variable @code{fsdebug} to 1 will allow the user to see
the fsimp function operations, list selection of simplification routines, etc.
With @code{fsdebug} at 1 or more, each candidate rejected by the
equivalence check is printed together with the routine that produced it,
and a note is printed when the assumptions leave too few test points, in
which case the check samples without them. fullsimp also reports when
it splits an expression into independent groups (see @code{fs_split}),
and when the combined result fails the check, in which case it
simplifies the expression whole, and it prints the rank (see
@code{fs_rank}) of the input and of the result. At 2 or more, the beam
search also prints the pool sizes after each round and why it stopped.
@end defvr

@anchor{fs_check}
@defvr {Variable} fs_check
Default value: @code{true}

When @code{fs_check} is @code{true}, @code{fullsimp} only returns
expressions equal in value to its input. It evaluates the input and each
candidate at the same test points and rejects a candidate that disagrees
at any of them. The test points are chosen so that every pair of
variables takes every combination of sign and quadrant, and they follow
the @code{assume} database: a variable with @code{assume(x > 0)} is only
sampled at positive values, so rewrites that hold for positive @code{x}
are accepted. Functions with no numeric definition, such as an undefined
@code{f(x)} or a quoted @code{'f(x)}, are replaced by fixed stand-in
functions for the comparison. Values follow Maxima's own conventions, so
an odd root of a negative number is its real root: @code{(-8)^(1/3)} is
@code{-2}, and a rewrite of @code{%e^(log(x)/3)} to @code{x^(1/3)} is
rejected because the two differ for negative @code{x}.

When the input cannot be evaluated numerically at all, for example
because it contains an unevaluated integral, only candidates produced by
routines that preserve value everywhere (such as @code{ratsimp},
@code{factor} and @code{trigsimp}) are accepted. Routines added through
@code{fs_custom_simp} never count as preserving value.

When fewer than half the test points can be drawn under the
assumptions, for example because they are contradictory or hard to
satisfy, the check uses points that ignore the assumptions instead.

The check is numeric, so it cannot see differences confined to isolated
points. @code{(x^2-1)/(x-1)} still simplifies to @code{x+1}, as it does
elsewhere in Maxima.

Setting @code{fs_check} to @code{false} turns the check off and gives
the results of earlier versions.
@end defvr

@anchor{fs_search}
@defvr {Variable} fs_search
Default value: @code{beam}

Selects the search @code{fullsimp} uses. With @code{beam}, it runs the
beam search described in the introduction, bounded by
@code{fs_beam_width} and @code{fs_max_rounds}. With @code{sweep}, it
runs the search of earlier versions: two passes over the routine list,
keeping a single current expression. Any other value is an error.
@end defvr

@anchor{fs_beam_width}
@defvr {Variable} fs_beam_width
Default value: @code{4}

The number of candidates the beam search keeps in its pool: the input,
@code{fs_beam_width - 2} of the cheapest candidates found so far, and one
candidate up to 80% larger than the cheapest. With a width of 2 the pool
holds the input and the cheapest candidate. With a width of 1 it holds
only the cheapest candidate, so the search climbs greedily from the
input. Larger widths explore more alternatives and take longer. The
value must be a positive integer.
@end defvr

@anchor{fs_max_rounds}
@defvr {Variable} fs_max_rounds
Default value: @code{4}

The largest number of rounds the beam search runs. It usually stops
earlier, as soon as a round adds no new candidate to the pool. The value
must be a positive integer.
@end defvr

@anchor{fs_split}
@defvr {Variable} fs_split
Default value: @code{true}

When @code{fs_split} is @code{true} and the expression is a sum or
product whose terms fall into two or more groups that share no
variables, @code{fullsimp} simplifies each group on its own and combines
the results. Terms with no variables, such as a constant @code{1}, may
go with any group: each group either keeps its own result or is
simplified again with all the constants added, and a group containing a
trigonometric or hyperbolic function also tries @code{+1} and @code{-1},
with the rest of the constant added back. The cheapest combination is
kept. So @code{1 - sin(x)^2 + tan(y)^2 + 1} gives
@code{cos(x)^2 + sec(y)^2}, and @code{cosh(z)^2 - sin(x)^2} gives
@code{sinh(z)^2 + cos(x)^2}. A group that simplifies to a constant joins
the constants. Afterwards @code{logcontract} and @code{rootscontract} are
tried on the combination, which merges forms like @code{log(a) + log(b)}
across groups.

Splitting keeps large independent parts from slowing each other down:
@code{fullsimp(expand((a+b+c+d+u)^10) + sin(x)^2 + cos(x)^2)} returns
@code{(a+b+c+d+u)^10 + 1} in well under a second. For the same reason a
group larger than 1000 (in the size @code{fullsimp} uses) takes no
shift. The price is that factorisations across groups are not
found: @code{(x+1)^2 - y^2} is not turned into @code{(x+1-y)*(x+1+y)}.

Setting @code{fs_split} to @code{false} simplifies the expression as a
whole.
@end defvr

@anchor{fs_prefer}
@defvr {Variable} fs_prefer
Default value: @code{[]}

A list of form preferences. Each preference counts its violations in an
expression, and @code{fullsimp} ranks candidates by those counts, in
list order, before size: a candidate with fewer violations of an earlier
preference wins however large it is, and size only decides between
candidates with equal counts. With @code{[]}, the default, results are
the smallest forms as before. Every candidate still has to pass the
equivalence check (see @code{fs_check}).

The preferences are:

@table @code
@item rational_denominator
No radical in any denominator. While active, @code{fullsimp} also tries
the routine @code{fs_rationalize}, which is @code{ratsimp} with
@code{algebraic : true} and rationalises denominators. @code{1/(1+sqrt(2))} becomes @code{sqrt(2)-1}. A single
radical such as @code{1/sqrt(3)} stays, because Maxima itself turns
@code{sqrt(3)/3} back into @code{1/sqrt(3)}.
@item single_fraction
No sum with a term that has a denominator, and no fraction inside a
denominator. @code{x + 1/x} becomes @code{(x^2+1)/x}. Numeric
coefficients such as the @code{1/2} in @code{x/2} do not count.
@item factored
No sum that @code{factor} rewrites as a product or power. While active,
sums are not split into independent groups (see @code{fs_split}), so
@code{(x+1)^2 - y^2} becomes @code{-((y-x-1)*(y+x+1))}.
@item expanded
No product with a sum as a factor, and no sum raised to an integer power
above 1. While active, products are not split into independent groups,
so @code{(x+1)*(y+1)} becomes @code{x*y+y+x+1}.
@item [avoid, f1, f2, ...]
No call to any of the named functions.
@item sin_cos
No @code{tan}, @code{cot}, @code{sec} or @code{csc}, the same as
@code{[avoid, tan, cot, sec, csc]}. @code{tan(x) + cot(x)} becomes
@code{2/sin(2*x)} instead of @code{2*csc(2*x)}.
@item exponential
No trigonometric or hyperbolic function.
@item trigonometric
No @code{%e} raised to a power containing @code{%i}.
@end table

@code{single_fraction}, @code{factored} and @code{expanded} look at the
arithmetic shape of the expression and do not count inside function
arguments. The other preferences count everywhere. @code{factored} and
@code{expanded} cannot be used together, and neither can
@code{exponential} and @code{trigonometric}. An unknown entry is an
error.

The result of @code{fullsimp} is never ranked worse than its input, and
never ranked worse than any single routine applied to the input. With
preferences this means a result can be larger than the input. A
preference no routine can reach has no effect, and excluding the
routine a preference needs (by naming it in the call to
@code{fullsimp}, as in @code{fullsimp(e, fs_rationalize)}) leaves the
preference unreachable.

Setting @code{fsdebug} to 1 prints the rank of the input and of the
result.
@end defvr

@anchor{fs_rank}
@deffn {Function} fs_rank (@code{expr})
Returns the list of violation counts of @code{expr} for each entry of
@code{fs_prefer}, in order, followed by its size @code{fscost(expr)}.
@code{fullsimp} compares candidates by this list. With
@code{fs_prefer : [sin_cos, rational_denominator]},
@code{fs_rank(cot(x)/sqrt(2))} returns @code{[1, 1, fscost(cot(x)/sqrt(2))]}.

@code{fscost} computes the size as the Lisp @code{conssize} plus the
string length, counting each variable and each undefined function name
as one character. Redefining @code{fscost} changes the size used by both
searches.
@end deffn

@anchor{fullsimp}
@deffn {Function} fullsimp (@code{expr},[simp1,simp2,...])
The @code{fullsimp} function takes an input expression @code{expr} and
attempts to find a simpler form using the builtin simplification
routines in core Maxima. These include: @code{[resimplify, expand,
combine, radcan, ratsimp, rootscontract, xthru, multthru, factor,
sqrtdenest, triglist,exptlist, loglist]}. Additional trigonometric
simplifications are applied as well. The full list of simplifications
is shown in the variable @code{simplist} which will be shown if
the variable @code{fsdebug} is set to 1.

There are two ways to manipulate the simplifications in @code{simplist}.
First, the option arguments to @code{fullsimp}, @code{simp1, simp2,
etc.} are simplification routines the user wants @emph{excluded} from
@code{simplist}.

Second, the user may create a @emph{list} called @code{fs_custom_simp},
and place any user defined simplification routines. For example the
code

@code{fs_custom_simp:[lambda([q], block([algebraic : true], ratsimp(q)))]}

will create a simplification routine where the variable @code{algebraic} is
set to @code{true}, while the @code{ratsimp} simplification is applied.

In general, @code{fullsimp} tries to find the smallest equivalent
expression based on the Common Lisp @code{conssize} and string size of
an expression, counting each variable and each undefined function name
as one character.

The user may manipulate the order of simplifications through
use of the exclusion option and the custom simplification option.

@code{fullsimp} is a simplifying function and can be added to the
command line as shown in the last example below.
@end deffn

@example
@group
(%i1) load("fsimp.mac")$ 
(%i2) fullsimp((1+cos(t))/sin(t)); 
                                        t
(%o2)                               cot(-)
                                        2
(%i3) fullsimp(cos(x)+%i*sin(x)); 
                                      %i x
(%o3)                               %e
(%i4) (cos(t)+1)/((%i*sin(p)-cos(p))*sin(t)),fullsimp; 
                                    %i p     t
(%o4)                           - %e     cot(-)
                                             2
@end group
@end example

The equivalence check keeps rewrites that are not valid for all
@code{x} out of the result, unless assumptions make them valid.

@example
@group
(%i1) load("fsimp.mac")$
(%i2) fullsimp(sqrt(x^2+2*x+1));
                                       2
(%o2)                        sqrt(x  + 2 x + 1)
(%i3) assume(x>-1);
(%o3)                              [x > - 1]
(%i4) fullsimp(sqrt(x^2+2*x+1));
(%o4)                               x + 1
@end group
@end example

Form preferences take priority over size.

@example
@group
(%i1) load("fsimp.mac")$
(%i2) fullsimp(x/(sqrt(x)+1));
                                     x
(%o2)                           -----------
                                sqrt(x) + 1
(%i3) fs_prefer : [rational_denominator];
(%o3)                       [rational_denominator]
(%i4) fullsimp(x/(sqrt(x)+1));
                                   3/2
                                  x    - x
(%o4)                             --------
                                   x - 1
(%i5) fs_prefer : [];
(%o5)                                 []
@end group
@end example

If you wish to exclude particular simplification routines,
add them to the arguments of fullsimp as shown below.

@example
@group
(%i1) load("fsimp.mac")$ 
(%i2) fullsimp((1+cos(t))/sin(t),fstrigsimp,trigsimp,trigreduce); 
                                  cos(t) + 1
(%o2)                             ----------
                                    sin(t)
@end group
@end example


